1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
 Let's have a look at some cost estimation techniques.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:08,040
 What we see here is also in the handbook.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:12,080
 The simplest method for cost estimation probably is the per unit method.

4
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,160
 You see the formula there.

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:16,839
 We have a unit cost factor and a number of units.

6
00:00:16,839 --> 00:00:22,400
 For instance, the cost of a house construction per liveable square foot is 150 dollars per

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,240
 square foot.

8
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:27,440
 The cost per parking space in a parking garage.

9
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:32,320
 The cost of construction is standard with suburban street per mile.

10
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:36,079
 So we have very rough calculations based on a unit cost.

11
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:41,280
 It's good for a first feasibility check, for instance.

12
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,680
 Well, simple examples.

13
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:49,719
 By the way, all the upcoming examples are pretty simple and straightforward.

14
00:00:49,719 --> 00:00:58,920
 So here we have a manufacturing process for sections of high pressure gas pipes.

15
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:03,079
 And we know that a 20 percent estimate is acceptable.

16
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:07,560
 So that means that we don't have to go into very detailed cost estimation.

17
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:10,519
 So the unit cost is probably good enough.

18
00:01:10,519 --> 00:01:12,920
 And then you see, well, what we need.

19
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:17,879
 We need materials, we need machinery tooling, we need labor for casting, for finishing,

20
00:01:17,879 --> 00:01:22,799
 for some indirect labor, for the provision, for instance.

21
00:01:22,799 --> 00:01:25,879
 We see the amount that we need in tons in hours.

22
00:01:25,879 --> 00:01:29,159
 We see the unit cost per ton or per hour.

23
00:01:29,159 --> 00:01:35,239
 And then we just have to multiply, of course, the amount in times the U, the cost, and then

24
00:01:35,239 --> 00:01:36,840
 we make the sum.

25
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:42,799
 So it's a very simple, very rough method for making an estimate.

26
00:01:42,799 --> 00:01:49,120
 So these are the cost indices or maybe the best known way of estimating costs, as they

27
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:55,159
 also appear in our daily life with the example of the consumer price index.

28
00:01:55,159 --> 00:01:56,319
 So this is a cost index.

29
00:01:56,319 --> 00:01:58,560
 It's a dimensionless number.

30
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:03,439
 It's the ratio of the cost of something today to its cost sometime in the past.

31
00:02:03,439 --> 00:02:10,560
 So besides our daily life example, there are a lot of industrial examples like the CE or

32
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:17,879
 the Plant Cost Index for Chemical Engineering or the Marshall and Swift Index for Equipment.

33
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:23,920
 This is an example for the Consumption Price Index for Belgium.

34
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:25,439
 You can look it up if you want.

35
00:02:25,439 --> 00:02:32,240
 You have the website where you can find the information.

36
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,319
 This is an example from the US.

37
00:02:35,359 --> 00:02:43,719
 I give it here because these systems are carefully monitored and if necessary, they are adjusted.

38
00:02:43,719 --> 00:02:51,599
 So then the new system for the consumer price index is described carefully so that everybody

39
00:02:51,599 --> 00:02:56,519
 knows exactly what is in it and what is not.

40
00:02:56,519 --> 00:02:59,319
 This is an example from some cost indices taken from the book.

41
00:02:59,319 --> 00:03:01,560
 We have the Chemical Engineering Plant Index.

42
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,680
 We have here our Consumer Price Index, the Crude Oil Index and then Pumps, Compressors

43
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:08,719
 and Equipment Index.

44
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:12,000
 We will see some more later like here.

45
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,920
 We have here Chemical Engineering, Engineering Cost and the Marshall and Swift.

46
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:18,520
 We see that it's rather simple.

47
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:30,400
 We have the cost at time t is a cost at time c0 multiplied by the ratio i t over i 0.

48
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:41,319
 The example seems to be a little bit outdated but the principle remains the same.

49
00:03:41,319 --> 00:03:47,480
 So here is a simple example.

50
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:53,360
 In evaluating the feasibility of a major construction project, an engineer is interested in estimating

51
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:56,800
 the cost of skilled labor for the job.

52
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:01,400
 The engineer finds that a project of similar complexity and magnitude was completed five

53
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:06,480
 years ago at a skilled labor cost of 360,000.

54
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:18,600
 The ENR, the Engineering News Record Skilled Labor Index was 3,496 then and now it's 4,038.

55
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:24,920
 So what is it today in terms of an estimation for the skilled labor cost?

56
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:30,800
 Well, if we use that equation, we see that we have making the simple calculation, we

57
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:35,160
 have our new estimate.

58
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,600
 This is another example.

59
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:42,480
 So a manufacturing engineer with Hughes Industries is in the process of estimating costs for

60
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,600
 plant expansion.

61
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:52,879
 Two important elements used in the manufacturing process are circuit boards and platinum alloy.

62
00:04:52,879 --> 00:05:00,079
 What the purchasing department did was they did spot checks of the market prices of these

63
00:05:00,079 --> 00:05:06,639
 two elements and they summarized things in the table you see below.

64
00:05:06,639 --> 00:05:12,959
 And then the question is, if you make the first quarter of 2001 the base period, what

65
00:05:12,959 --> 00:05:17,600
 are then the cost indices for the other periods?

66
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:20,819
 So if you have a look here at what is in the red frame, it's easy.

67
00:05:20,819 --> 00:05:28,779
 So we take Q1 being the base, so that means that for Q3 of the previous year, we get

68
00:05:28,779 --> 00:05:41,740
 56.7 divided by the 56.6 of our base quarter that gives us 100.17 and similar way we do

69
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:43,620
 all the rest.

70
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:48,620
 Why are these indices important or why are they interesting?

71
00:05:49,220 --> 00:05:56,000
 It helps us to see variability, it helps us to see trends, it helps us to see how the

72
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,699
 market is behaving.

73
00:05:57,699 --> 00:06:01,980
 So it's providing us some management information.

74
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:09,120
 A note, maybe first, which is not on the slide, but it's important to note that these indices

75
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:11,399
 are published, so you don't have to compute them yourself.

76
00:06:11,399 --> 00:06:16,699
 They are published like the Chemical Engineering Index or the Marshall and Swift Index or the

77
00:06:16,899 --> 00:06:21,319
 Engineering News Record Index that are published.

78
00:06:21,319 --> 00:06:25,939
 They are built up out of components.

79
00:06:25,939 --> 00:06:30,740
 These components can have subcomponents and each of these subcomponents and components

80
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:34,060
 have different weights that are taken into account.

81
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:43,740
 Also every index has a base here that's not the same for all indices.

82
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:53,420
 This is an example of the Chemical Engineering Index and the little table or list on the

83
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:55,620
 right gives you an impression what is in it.

84
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:59,780
 We have an Equipment Index, we have a Construction Labor Index, we have a Buildings Index and

85
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:03,060
 we have an Engineering and Supervision Index.

86
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:06,340
 If you see that or the components, there are subcomponents.

87
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:11,420
 If you see for the Equipment Index, we have heat exchanges, we have process machinery,

88
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:16,500
 pipes, valves and fittings, process instruments, pumps and compressors, electrical equipment,

89
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,379
 structural support and miscellaneous.

90
00:07:18,379 --> 00:07:28,379
 So we have equipment that is actually used a lot in chemical engineering plants.

91
00:07:28,379 --> 00:07:34,660
 Well to the right you see some more details, you see also the weights and what you see

92
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:38,220
 in the graph is how it's evolving over time.

93
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:45,620
 It's not monotonically evolving, the curves are increasing, everything gets more expensive.

94
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:50,540
 It means that there are ups and downs like for instance if there's an economic crisis

95
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:55,460
 or if there's a lot of inflation and things like that, it can influence that curve.

96
00:07:55,460 --> 00:08:01,500
 You also see that the different curves are not evolving in the same way.

97
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:06,580
 So that's also something that is important to note.

98
00:08:06,579 --> 00:08:12,620
 Evolution, so there were some revisions, so every now and then it's needed to revise

99
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:18,139
 the index and that's what the Chemical Engineering Index is doing.

100
00:08:18,139 --> 00:08:24,139
 Its origin is in 1963, so it's already quite an old index.

101
00:08:24,139 --> 00:08:29,019
 It remained the same till 1982.

102
00:08:29,019 --> 00:08:35,500
 In 2002 there was a last major revision, so if you see every 20 years we can think that

103
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:40,019
 one might be coming up soon.

104
00:08:40,019 --> 00:08:44,220
 This is a nice, I would definitely tell you to read it.

105
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:46,659
 It's a quite interesting story.

106
00:08:46,659 --> 00:08:53,539
 It's saying observing the five years rule, so I would suggest stop the recording and

107
00:08:53,539 --> 00:08:56,139
 read it.

108
00:08:56,139 --> 00:08:57,139
 What is it about?

109
00:08:57,139 --> 00:09:06,699
 It's about, so we know that in 1970 a liquid oxygen plant of 700 tons a day had an initial

110
00:09:06,699 --> 00:09:09,179
 cost of 10 million.

111
00:09:09,179 --> 00:09:15,559
 Now we want to know in 2000, 30 years later, what a liquid oxygen plant of the same capacity

112
00:09:15,559 --> 00:09:17,019
 would cost.

113
00:09:17,019 --> 00:09:25,139
 So we can apply our cost index formula and then we find that it would be 31.3 million.

114
00:09:25,139 --> 00:09:26,220
 So is that okay?

115
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:31,180
 Well, we applied the cost index formula correctly, so okay.

116
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,180
 But it's not okay.

117
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:35,580
 There is a need for critical reflection.

118
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:42,420
 We can't just extrapolate that index so far.

119
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:50,980
 In 30 years a lot can happen, like here the liquid oxygen plant, there were a lot of changes

120
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:56,200
 in production process, production efficiency went up, so technological changes.

121
00:09:57,180 --> 00:10:02,520
 Also, occupational health and safety legislation changed, requiring modifications to the equipment,

122
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:06,800
 requiring extra monitoring equipment, requiring extra personnel.

123
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:11,840
 Also equipment and labor rates, two components that are important in that cost index, evolved

124
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:15,759
 at different rates, so we need a different weighting.

125
00:10:15,759 --> 00:10:20,000
 So we could say that the cost index that we use is not reliable anymore.

126
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,160
 So we should be careful with extended time extrapolations.

127
00:10:23,159 --> 00:10:28,839
 For some sectors that might be okay, but for others it's definitely not okay.

128
00:10:28,839 --> 00:10:29,839
 So be careful.

129
00:10:29,839 --> 00:10:37,719
 That's why they say the five-year rules, don't use them more than five years ahead.

130
00:10:37,719 --> 00:10:41,719
 A little bit of information on that engineering cost index.

131
00:10:41,719 --> 00:10:49,039
 There's an engineering use record construction cost index and engineering use record building

132
00:10:49,039 --> 00:10:50,039
 cost index.

133
00:10:50,039 --> 00:10:55,000
 Both are containing construction costs, but there's a difference in labor costs.

134
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 The CCI is more for projects where labor costs are very important, why the building costs

135
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:09,199
 is more suited for building projects with a lot of structures in it.

136
00:11:09,199 --> 00:11:11,719
 And here you see some information.

137
00:11:11,719 --> 00:11:14,559
 So the base here is already very old index.

138
00:11:14,559 --> 00:11:18,679
 They are working with spot prices from a single source.

139
00:11:18,679 --> 00:11:22,759
 It needs to be a published source.

140
00:11:22,759 --> 00:11:25,279
 They're looking at 20 cities.

141
00:11:25,279 --> 00:11:30,679
 They make an average, so not a weighted average, an unweighted average.

142
00:11:30,679 --> 00:11:36,639
 There is no Arizona or Florida city in it.

143
00:11:36,639 --> 00:11:40,719
 There's no seasonal adjustment.

144
00:11:40,719 --> 00:11:43,479
 So it's an index.

145
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:49,560
 I mean, don't expect these index to be 100% accurate and taking into account all different

146
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:54,720
 factors and influences that you want or expect.

147
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:56,519
 So be careful if you use an index.

148
00:11:56,519 --> 00:12:04,879
 They're very handy, but be careful that you know what exactly is in it and what's not.

149
00:12:04,879 --> 00:12:05,879
 This is a study.

150
00:12:05,879 --> 00:12:11,159
 So it's a bit outdated, but it's still an interesting problem situation.

151
00:12:11,159 --> 00:12:19,959
 So a chemical company in Belgium wanted to use an index, but they were not very keen

152
00:12:19,959 --> 00:12:23,319
 on using an index from literature.

153
00:12:23,319 --> 00:12:27,199
 And here you see these indices.

154
00:12:27,199 --> 00:12:31,959
 They were brought to the same base here to compare them.

155
00:12:31,959 --> 00:12:38,000
 And what they noticed was that there was a large difference between these indices.

156
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:44,519
 They weren't too confident that it was a good idea to use either of them.

157
00:12:44,519 --> 00:12:49,200
 So they wanted to use one themselves and they wanted to have a quick one.

158
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:54,639
 So they simplified the cost index formula and they did some simulations so that they

159
00:12:54,639 --> 00:12:59,279
 ended up with three factors and three weighting factors.

160
00:12:59,279 --> 00:13:04,720
 Now it's, well, I say the chemical company in Belgium, but actually it was a multinational

161
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:10,639
 and they tried to apply this formula to different countries and they came out with different

162
00:13:10,639 --> 00:13:15,639
 curves, very different results.

163
00:13:15,639 --> 00:13:22,920
 So they said, well, if you have to use that in computations in Belgium, we will have to

164
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:26,779
 see where our materials come from, where the equipment coming from.

165
00:13:26,779 --> 00:13:33,200
 So they made it a weighted sum of country index indices based on where their material

166
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:35,800
 was coming from.

167
00:13:35,800 --> 00:13:41,920
 So it's giving you some idea on how these spaces are built.

168
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:44,920
 Now why did they want so a quick index?

169
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:50,560
 Well, they were using for their maintenance, they were using the formula total maintenance

170
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:57,120
 cost per year over capital replacement value or the stores ratio, MRO stands for maintenance,

171
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:01,560
 repair and operating supply over capital replacement value.

172
00:14:01,559 --> 00:14:09,159
 The capital replacement value is defined as the money needed today to replace a current

173
00:14:09,159 --> 00:14:11,839
 installation.

174
00:14:11,839 --> 00:14:19,519
 They didn't want to go through a very sophisticated detailed engineering calculation to determine

175
00:14:19,519 --> 00:14:21,039
 that capital replacement.

176
00:14:21,039 --> 00:14:24,199
 They just wanted to use a index.

177
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:34,480
 So that's why they wanted that index that was really tailored to their specific situation.

178
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:41,800
 They switched from the old index that they were using to the new cost index.

179
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:49,240
 You see that the trend is fairly similar, but you also see that the numbers are not

180
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:50,919
 the same.

181
00:14:50,919 --> 00:15:00,600
 So you might be surprised at the evolution of the numbers if you do not know that another

182
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:01,680
 index has been used.

183
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:06,919
 So again, be careful with the use of these indices.

184
00:15:06,919 --> 00:15:10,159
 Here they used it for the stores ratio.

185
00:15:10,159 --> 00:15:15,120
 Okay, but let's move on.

186
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:17,600
 These are some other examples.

187
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:22,360
 For instance, for material handling equipment manufacturing.

188
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:27,879
 This is one from the ICRI Institute for medical equipment.

189
00:15:27,879 --> 00:15:34,759
 Okay, this is something we will do in the exercises now.

190
00:15:34,759 --> 00:15:37,040
 What benefits do you see?

191
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:39,360
 What challenges do you see by using cost indices?

192
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:46,240
 If you recall in the first open office, this type of question was raised as being a typical

193
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:54,000
 another typical type of case study that you could come across in the exam.

194
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:02,600
 Now cost estimation relationships, also called CERs or parametric methods.

195
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:07,000
 They use design variables to predict costs.

196
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:13,200
 So they're different from cost index methods because the index is based on cost history

197
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:15,720
 of a well-defined quantity and quality of a variable.

198
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:16,720
 There are two types.

199
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:20,400
 We have the cost capacity equation and the factor methods.

200
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:22,200
 Let's have a look.

201
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:26,440
 So cost capacity equations are the most widely used ones.

202
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:30,720
 They're also called power laws or sizing models.

203
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:33,860
 And the formula is simple, as you see.

204
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:42,860
 So C2, the cost of at capacity Q2, we estimate that by taking the cost of capacity at Q2

205
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,600
 Q1.

206
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:47,200
 And then we divide that.

207
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:53,240
 We multiply by Q2 over Q1 raised to a certain power.

208
00:16:53,240 --> 00:16:55,520
 Now that certain power, where do we find it?

209
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:57,640
 Well, we find that it's published.

210
00:16:57,640 --> 00:17:05,559
 For instance, in chemical engineering's handbook, the US Environmental Protection Agency handbooks,

211
00:17:05,559 --> 00:17:07,759
 professional journals, etc.

212
00:17:07,759 --> 00:17:11,920
 An average X is 0.6.

213
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:15,360
 If you have an X smaller than 1, we have economies of scale.

214
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:17,680
 If X is 1, we have a linear relationship.

215
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:21,880
 If X is larger than 1, we have these economies of scale.

216
00:17:21,880 --> 00:17:32,880
 Of course, we could complement this by having our cost indices also in the picture.

217
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:35,920
 So here you see some examples.

218
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:44,920
 Maybe to the right you see that indeed if we have C1 of 1000, Q1 of 1000, and then Q2 of 2000.

219
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:46,920
 And what is C2?

220
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:51,560
 If X is 1, well, we have C2 is 2000, so it's linear.

221
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:55,000
 If X is smaller than 1, we have our economies of scale.

222
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,440
 If it's larger, we have these economies of scale.

223
00:17:58,440 --> 00:18:03,840
 Now here you see some examples of components and you see the size range.

224
00:18:03,839 --> 00:18:08,439
 And then you see the exponent X that you are supposed to use.

225
00:18:08,439 --> 00:18:10,119
 So here a simple example.

226
00:18:10,119 --> 00:18:14,919
 So we're talking about a digester.

227
00:18:14,919 --> 00:18:21,919
 We know that for one capable of handling a flow rate of 0.5 million gallons per day,

228
00:18:21,919 --> 00:18:25,279
 the cost was 1.7 million in 2000.

229
00:18:25,279 --> 00:18:34,000
 And now we are asked to estimate the cost for a flow rate of 2.0 million of gallons per day.

230
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:40,200
 The exponent we have to use is in the range 0.2 to 40.

231
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:45,039
 So it's 0.14 in previous table.

232
00:18:45,039 --> 00:18:53,440
 And the cost indices that we have to use or the cost index rather in 2000 is 131.

233
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:58,200
 And it has been updated for this year to 125.

234
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:07,240
 So in blue, we have our cost estimation based on the capacity.

235
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:12,039
 We're talking about the same piece of equipment, but they have a different capacity.

236
00:19:12,039 --> 00:19:13,200
 So this is the first part.

237
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:18,960
 And then the second part with dotted lines is then the cost index that we are applying.

238
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:20,240
 So we can combine both.

239
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:25,240
 Another CER method is the factor method.

240
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:29,839
 It was developed originally for estimating total plant cost.

241
00:19:29,839 --> 00:19:37,640
 The thing is that the total plant cost will be estimated based on major equipment items used in that plant.

242
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:39,960
 And then we multiply with factors.

243
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:46,759
 So we assume that the major costs or the cost of major equipment is already available.

244
00:19:47,359 --> 00:19:51,480
 And then we can use the proper factors to have a rapid estimate.

245
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:58,920
 These factors are called the Leng factors because the first to use these factors was Leng.

246
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:01,680
 See, also not very recent.

247
00:20:01,680 --> 00:20:04,319
 There have been updates in the method.

248
00:20:04,319 --> 00:20:13,079
 Basic formula is CT, the total plant cost is based on CE, which is the cost of major equipment in that plant,

249
00:20:13,079 --> 00:20:20,399
 times H and H is a factor, cost factor, which is a sum of individual cost factors.

250
00:20:22,879 --> 00:20:36,879
 For instance, an engineer once has learned that the expansion of a solid fluid process plant is expected to have a delivered equipment cost of 1.5 million.

251
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:43,200
 If the overall cost factor for this type of plant is 3.63, then estimate the total plant cost.

252
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:49,200
 And that's not a very difficult equation, of course.

253
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:58,600
 Now, these factors, well, Leng showed that direct cost factors and indirect cost factors can be combined into one overall factor.

254
00:20:58,719 --> 00:21:09,559
 And we know what they are for some types of plants, 3.1, for instance, for a solid process plant or 4.74 for fluid process plants.

255
00:21:09,559 --> 00:21:11,319
 So that's what you see on top.

256
00:21:11,319 --> 00:21:19,399
 So we have H is 1 plus all these factors, and these factors include direct and indirect costs.

257
00:21:19,399 --> 00:21:21,919
 That method has been refined.

258
00:21:21,919 --> 00:21:27,059
 And that's the second equation that you see.

259
00:21:27,059 --> 00:21:36,059
 There we have, again, the 1 plus the sum of all if i's, but these if i's are direct cost components.

260
00:21:36,059 --> 00:21:42,480
 And then we multiply that by 1 plus if big i, and that big i is the indirect cost factor.

261
00:21:42,480 --> 00:21:44,079
 So it's a slightly different approach.

262
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:56,379
 The delivered equipment cost for a small chemical process plant is expected to be 2 million if the direct cost factor is 1.61.

263
00:21:56,380 --> 00:22:02,900
 And the indirect cost factor is 0.25, determine the total cost for the plant.

264
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:07,400
 And we will just add up all the cost factors.

265
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:11,240
 So H is 1 plus the direct one and the indirect one.

266
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:18,400
 We multiply the 2 million with that and we end up with 5,720,000.

267
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:20,300
 Let's have a look at another example.

268
00:22:20,300 --> 00:22:24,740
 So we're talking about an activated sludge wastewater treatment plant.

269
00:22:24,799 --> 00:22:30,500
 And we have estimates for the delivered equipment first costs.

270
00:22:30,500 --> 00:22:39,700
 And we also know that the cost factor for installation of piping, concrete, steel, insulation, supports, etc. is 0.49.

271
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:45,460
 The construction factor is 0.53 and indirect cost factor is 0.21.

272
00:22:45,460 --> 00:22:52,940
 And we are asked to determine the total plant cost if A, all cost factors are applied to the cost of the delivered equipment.

273
00:22:52,940 --> 00:22:57,740
 And B, the indirect cost factor is applied to the total direct cost.

274
00:22:57,740 --> 00:23:00,059
 So again, not very difficult.

275
00:23:00,059 --> 00:23:06,340
 So in A, we just add up the 0.49, 0.53 and the 0.21 with 1.

276
00:23:06,340 --> 00:23:08,539
 And then we have our H.

277
00:23:08,539 --> 00:23:18,960
 In the second case, B, we just add up the direct cost factors and we come to 2.02.

278
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:23,680
 And then we multiply that with 1 plus the 0.21.

279
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:25,980
 So we have a slightly different number.

280
00:23:25,980 --> 00:23:30,380
 In the first case, we had 608,790.

281
00:23:30,380 --> 00:23:38,380
 Second case, we have the 667,267.

282
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:40,860
 So they are quite different.

283
00:23:40,859 --> 00:23:50,659
 So it's important to know that if you use factors, what exactly is in there and is that suitable for what you want to do?

284
00:23:50,659 --> 00:23:56,079
 An issue on indirect costs, I will not talk a lot about that because it's management accounting.

285
00:23:56,079 --> 00:24:03,919
 I expect that most of you have seen indirect costs in a management accounting course.

286
00:24:03,919 --> 00:24:09,819
 Sometimes we have to rely on ABC casting, activity based casting.

287
00:24:09,819 --> 00:24:13,019
 Just very briefly what it's about.

288
00:24:13,019 --> 00:24:20,079
 The setting is a third party warehouse and assume that warehousing costs like building, maintenance, equipment, packaging materials,

289
00:24:20,079 --> 00:24:22,720
 personnel or 300,000 per year.

290
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:28,919
 And we have three customers and each customer has a contract for 1500 pallet positions.

291
00:24:28,919 --> 00:24:35,720
 So it's the storage square meters that they take in and that's also all the incoming and

292
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:41,779
 outgoing movements that are related to their stock.

293
00:24:41,779 --> 00:24:49,120
 So at first, it might seem logical to split up the 300,000 evenly over the three customers in a euro per pallet position.

294
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:55,420
 So a very traditional way of computing indirect costs.

295
00:24:55,420 --> 00:24:58,120
 But maybe that's not so logical.

296
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:01,620
 It's only logical when the three customers have similar activities.

297
00:25:01,619 --> 00:25:11,419
 If, for instance, we have a situation where customers A and B always have reception and shipments in full pallets,

298
00:25:11,419 --> 00:25:18,119
 but for customers C, only the receipts are in full pallets, but the shipping occurs in individual boxes.

299
00:25:18,119 --> 00:25:24,279
 So that means that C has need for a lot more manipulations in the warehouse.

300
00:25:24,279 --> 00:25:30,079
 So it seems fair that also that then C pays more than A and B.

301
00:25:30,079 --> 00:25:37,919
 But in order to determine how much more that C should pay, we should make a more detailed analysis on the warehousing activities.

302
00:25:37,919 --> 00:25:43,879
 And that exactly is what activity based costing is doing.

303
00:25:43,879 --> 00:25:47,519
 Exercises, we have a separate section on exercises.

304
00:25:47,519 --> 00:25:53,079
 If you go back to the indirect cost analysis, this is a very interesting example in the book.

305
00:25:53,079 --> 00:25:56,720
 So it's an indirect cost analysis of medical equipment and manufacturing costs.

306
00:25:56,720 --> 00:26:05,519
 So for those of you who have not had been in contact with indirect costs, it's definitely something an activity based costing.

307
00:26:05,519 --> 00:26:10,400
 It's definitely interesting to have a look at it.

308
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:15,759
 I summarized the solution for you.

309
00:26:15,759 --> 00:26:21,700
 We will not discuss it. It will not be part of the exam, but it's definitely interesting to have a look at.

310
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:25,220
 OK, that brings us to the end of this chapter.

311
00:26:25,220 --> 00:26:28,900
 Cost estimation, what is it?

312
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:32,420
 What about accuracy? Learning curve.

313
00:26:32,420 --> 00:26:36,360
 Very important. Maybe I should have underlined it because it's something extra.

314
00:26:36,360 --> 00:26:39,900
 So don't forget to study it. It's not in handbook.

315
00:26:39,900 --> 00:26:42,620
 And then we have the cost estimation. We saw some techniques.

316
00:26:42,620 --> 00:26:49,160
 The per unit method, the cost indices, the cost capacity equations with the power law, with the factor method.

317
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:55,100
 Of course, we did not talk about it, but if you don't want to go with these cost estimation methods,

318
00:26:55,100 --> 00:27:04,340
 you could rely on data from professional organizations or you could buy some design software

319
00:27:04,340 --> 00:27:12,600
 or you could do some detailed engineering calculations that depends on how accurate your estimation needs to be.

320
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:15,100
 And then, well, at the end, we mentioned indirect costs,

321
00:27:15,099 --> 00:27:19,579
 but that's something that is a bit outside the scope of this course.

